{"author":{"id":"2de4467f26c42fb223f4d3ddd5611e7c0513d3daae4c1dc315de039036bc997d"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1778952319,"nonce":"jMEOxRpEILTahuKNpbvkADSAz3s=","title":"Reserve dimensions for images and figures","message":"## Problem\nImages and figure components generally render without explicit width/height, sizes, or aspect-ratio reservation. This can cause layout shift and makes browser image selection less informed.\n\nExamples:\n- `Figure` renders raw `\u003cimg\u003e` tags without width/height or loading policy.\n- `PostImage` emits responsive `\u003cpicture\u003e` sources but no `sizes` or intrinsic dimensions.\n- `gen-images.mjs` reads source dimensions but does not persist them into the image manifest.\n- Series SVG/asset images are copied to public and rendered directly, outside the raster optimization manifest path.\n\n## Context\nMany assets are diagrams and SVGs, so layout shift may be modest in practice, but the site would be more predictable if image dimensions were part of the content/rendering contract.\n\n## Potential Solutions\n- Extend the generated image manifest with source width/height and emit `width`, `height`, and `sizes` in `PostImage`.\n- Give `Figure` a server-rendered/static path where possible, with `loading=lazy` for below-the-fold figures.\n- Preserve SVG viewBox dimensions or infer aspect ratio for figure wrappers.\n- Add visual checks for figure captions and layout at desktop/mobile widths after the margin-note rewrite.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}